<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Boniface</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 675–754</span> · Also known as Boniface of Mainz, Winfrid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Boniface was an English monk, born Winfrid, who led the papal mission to the Germans. He worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, organised the dioceses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a>, Regensburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a>, and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> and the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a>. As archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> he reformed the Frankish church. He was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> in 754 and buried at Fulda.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-boniface.jpg" alt="Saint Boniface" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cornelis Bloemaert · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Boniface_by_Cornelis_Bloemaert.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
